Feature: Sales KPI Table Generation via PySpark Refactored Classes and Functions

  Background:
    Given the Unity Catalog tables "purgo_playground.product_data", "purgo_playground.product_sales_data", and "purgo_playground.product_marketshare_data" exist
    And the output table "purgo_playground.sales_kpi" exists with schema:
      | sales_year | int    |
      | sales_product_id | string |
      | product_name | string |
      | market_segment | string |
      | total_sales | bigint |
      | prev_year_sales | bigint |
      | yoy_growth_pct | double |
      | avg_market_share | double |
      | market_penetration_flag | string |
      | sales_rank | int |

  Scenario: Happy path - Generate sales KPI table with all required metrics
    Given product_data contains valid product_id, product_name, market_segment
    And product_sales_data contains valid sales_product_id, sales_amount, sales_date
    And product_marketshare_data contains valid ms_product_id, market_share_pct
    When the refactored PySpark pipeline is executed
    Then the output table "purgo_playground.sales_kpi" is written in overwrite mode
    And each row contains:
      | sales_year | sales_product_id | product_name | market_segment | total_sales | prev_year_sales | yoy_growth_pct | avg_market_share | market_penetration_flag | sales_rank |
    And total_sales is the sum of sales_amount for each (sales_product_id, sales_year)
    And prev_year_sales is the total_sales for the previous year for the same sales_product_id, or null if not available
    And yoy_growth_pct is ((total_sales - prev_year_sales) / prev_year_sales) * 100, rounded to 2 decimals, or null if prev_year_sales is null
    And avg_market_share is the average market_share_pct for each (sales_product_id, sales_year), rounded to 2 decimals
    And market_penetration_flag is "High" if avg_market_share > 25, "Medium" if avg_market_share between 10 and 25, "Low" otherwise
    And sales_rank is the rank of total_sales within each sales_year, descending

  Scenario Outline: Validation - Required fields and types in input tables
    Given <table> contains a row with <field> missing or null
    When the pipeline is executed
    Then the row is excluded from KPI calculation
    Examples:
      | table                        | field             |
      | product_sales_data           | sales_amount      |
      | product_sales_data           | sales_date        |
      | product_data                 | product_id        |
      | product_marketshare_data     | market_share_pct  |

  Scenario Outline: Validation - Type mismatches in input tables
    Given <table> contains a row with <field> of incorrect type
    When the pipeline is executed
    Then an error is raised indicating type mismatch for <field>
    Examples:
      | table                        | field             |
      | product_sales_data           | sales_amount      |
      | product_marketshare_data     | market_share_pct  |
      | product_data                 | product_id        |

  Scenario Outline: Validation - Duplicate keys in input tables
    Given <table> contains duplicate rows for <key>
    When the pipeline is executed
    Then duplicates are aggregated as per groupBy logic
    Examples:
      | table                        | key                        |
      | product_sales_data           | sales_product_id,sales_year|
      | product_marketshare_data     | ms_product_id              |

  Scenario: Error path - Missing input table
    Given the table "purgo_playground.product_sales_data" does not exist
    When the pipeline is executed
    Then an error is raised: "Input table purgo_playground.product_sales_data not found"

  Scenario: Error path - Output table write failure
    Given the output table "purgo_playground.sales_kpi" is locked or unavailable
    When the pipeline attempts to write
    Then an error is raised: "Failed to write to purgo_playground.sales_kpi"

  Scenario Outline: Market Penetration Flag logic
    Given avg_market_share is <avg_market_share>
    When market_penetration_flag is calculated
    Then the value is <flag>
    Examples:
      | avg_market_share | flag    |
      | 30.0             | High    |
      | 25.0             | Medium  |
      | 10.0             | Medium  |
      | 9.99             | Low     |
      | null             | Low     |

  Scenario Outline: YoY Growth calculation
    Given total_sales is <total_sales> and prev_year_sales is <prev_year_sales>
    When yoy_growth_pct is calculated
    Then the value is <yoy_growth_pct>
    Examples:
      | total_sales | prev_year_sales | yoy_growth_pct |
      | 2000        | 1000            | 100.00         |
      | 1500        | 1500            | 0.00           |
      | 1000        | null            | null           |
      | 0           | 1000            | -100.00        |

  Scenario Outline: Sales Rank assignment
    Given sales_year is <sales_year> and total_sales values are <sales_list>
    When sales_rank is assigned
    Then the product with highest total_sales gets rank 1, next gets 2, etc.
    Examples:
      | sales_year | sales_list      | expected_ranks |
      | 2023       | [5000,4000,3000]| [1,2,3]        |
      | 2022       | [1000,1000,500] | [1,1,3]        |

  Scenario: Data cleaning and renaming
    Given product_sales_data contains column "product_id"
    When the pipeline is executed
    Then the column is renamed to "sales_product_id"
    And only rows with non-null sales_amount are processed

  Scenario: Joins and enrichment
    Given sales_product_id exists in product_data and product_marketshare_data
    When the pipeline is executed
    Then product_name and market_segment are joined from product_data
    And market_share_pct is joined from product_marketshare_data

  Scenario: Final output selection
    When the pipeline completes KPI calculation
    Then only the following columns are selected for output:
      | sales_year | sales_product_id | product_name | market_segment | total_sales | prev_year_sales | yoy_growth_pct | avg_market_share | market_penetration_flag | sales_rank |

  Scenario: Display step for interactive use
    When the pipeline completes
    Then the final DataFrame is displayed for review

  Scenario: Refactoring into classes and functions
    Given the original script logic
    When the code is refactored
    Then each logical block (data loading, cleaning, joining, aggregation, output) is encapsulated in a function or class method
    And the logic and output remain unchanged

# END
